================================================================================
shamsu/agents/simple_chat.py - _already_written(), used at the nudge site

Every prose nudge that fired on a code-editing case in the 240-attempt run was
false: 3/3 on repairs_a_file_it_cannot_pattern_match, 2/2 on
ask_before_choosing_an_approach - both in the flaky set. The live shape:

    [tool/write_file]  ok: Overwrote app.py (+106 -1 lines, 108 total)
    [tool/verify]      ok: Checked app.py: no syntax errors
    [assistant]        I've added comprehensive authentication support...
    [user]             You showed the new contents of app.py but did NOT
                       change the file. Apply it now: ...
    [assistant]        The file already has authentication implemented with:
                       - Password hashing  - Session management ...

`describes_an_unmade_edit(text, self._files)` was never told what the turn had
written. `changed` was in scope two lines above the call site all along.

================================================================================
R5 - "What tax rate does pricing.py apply?" was not recognised as a question
================================================================================
shamsu/agents/simple_chat.py - _OPENS_WITH_A_QUESTION, _POLITE_REQUEST,
asks_only_for_words() reordered

The prose nudge fired on the qa_reads_repo_fact prompt 10 times across 15
attempts, telling a turn whose whole deliverable was an answer to start
writing files.

Two things the tests caught while fixing it:
  * The question test has to run BEFORE the change-verb veto. The tax-rate
    sentence contains `apply`, which IS a change-verb, so the veto rejected the
    clearest question in the suite. Requiring an interrogative opener AND a
    closing "?" is what makes running it first safe.
  * "Can you rewrite parse_config?" is an instruction wearing a question mark
    and must NOT become words-only - that is the expensive direction. Hence
    _POLITE_REQUEST. It also exposed a pre-existing gap: `rewrite` and
    `replace` were missing from _CHANGE_VERBS entirely (matched on word
    boundaries, so `write` never covered `rewrite`).

    True   'What tax rate does pricing.py apply in its total() function?'
    True   'What does this function return?'
    True   'How should we approach the refactor?'
    True   'Can you explain what this does?'
    True   'Review the modules and tell me what they do.'
    False  'Can you rewrite parse_config?'
    False  'Could you add a test for this?'
    False  'What is broken? fix it'
    False  'review the code and fix the bug'
    False  'Add authentication to app.py.'

