THIRD BATCH - R9 (fixed), R10 (investigated, no independent defect)
================================================================================

R9 - the approval handover across NESTED prompts
  shamsu/cli/live_console.py - _handover_depth, depth-counted resume()

  I went looking for a wiring bug first and did NOT find one. Both edges are
  registered (repl.py:5028-5029, on_prompt_open(live.stand_down) /
  on_prompt_close(live.resume)), and reading_input() fires both, including on
  the exception path. The mechanism is sound, exactly as the post-mortem said.

  What IS wrong: `reading_input()` keeps a `_PROMPT_DEPTH` because one prompt
  can open inside another. The handover did not. With a nested prompt:
    * `_suspend_frame` overwrote `self._handover` with the inner waiter, so the
      OUTER waiter was never set and its `run_in_terminal` callable waited the
      full HANDOVER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS - 900s of a frame that does not come back.
    * the inner prompt closing released the frame while the OUTER question was
      still on screen, repainting the alternate screen over it.
  First suspend hands over, last resume takes it back. `set_frame(None)` forces
  a release at turn end so an unbalanced open cannot strand the terminal.

  NOT done: rendering the approval as a modal float inside the frame. That is
  the UX redesign the post-mortem describes and it still wants an interactive
  session; this fixes a correctness bug in what is already there.

R10 - half of all tool calls were re-reads. NO INDEPENDENT DEFECT FOUND.
  Three hypotheses tested against session 20260822-183305-4975, all refuted:

  1. "the dedup guard is broken"
     It fired 22 times in that session. `_already_sent_this_range` works.

  2. "elision wrongly clears _ranges_sent"
     Correct as written. The guard returns a NOTE saying "you already have
     lines X-Y"; once the payload is elided the model genuinely does not have
     it, so re-reading is legitimate and the clear is right.

  3. "MAX_PROTECTED_READ_PATHS = 4 is below the working set"
     Exactly 4 distinct files were read (asteroid.js 34, player.js 15,
     main.js 2, collision.js 2). The cap never bound.

  What the data shows is R6: 8 of 47 ranged reads were impossible, each
  answered with ok:true and one line. The read guards were firing correctly
  against a cause they could not address. Re-measure after R6 before touching
  any detector - tuning one now would tune it against a cause that is gone.

